":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1550751827-4bd374c3f58b?w=1200&h=600&fit=crop","alt":"Shanghai skyline at night","style":{"width":"100%","borderRadius":"12px","margin":"24px 0"}}],["$","p","59",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"*Shanghai's Lujiazui financial district — where many of China's AI consumer empires are headquartered. Image: Unsplash*"}]]}],["$","hr","61",{"style":{"border":"none","borderTop":"1px solid #1a1a1a","margin":"32px 0"}}],["$","h2","63",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Doubao's 100 Million DAU: The App That Ate China"}]]}],["$","p","65",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"ByteDance's Doubao launched in August 2023 as a relatively modest AI assistant — essentially a Chinese-language ChatGPT competitor with tight integration into ByteDance's content ecosystem. Less than three years later, it hit 100 million daily active users, a milestone that took TikTok years longer to achieve and that no Western AI-native app has approached."}]]}],["$","p","67",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The growth trajectory is worth examining in detail:"}]]}],["$","div","77",{"style":{"overflowX":"auto","margin":"24px 0"},"children":["$","table",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","borderCollapse":"collapse","fontSize":"14px"},"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Milestone"}]]}],["$","th","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","col